    "body": "## Description\n\n`validate_device` in `tests/unit/v1/zero/test_offload_states.py` requires the contiguous grad buffer to be **gone** once it is reported on cpu, because a cpu device means `offload_states()` moved it to the host tier:\n\n```python\nif state == OffloadStateTypeEnum.contiguous_grad_buffer and state_device[state] == torch.device(\"cpu\"):\n    assert len(get_state_devices(model, state)) == 0, f\"State {state} must be removed after offload_states()\"\n```\n\nOn the cpu accelerator the offload target **is** the accelerator, so `state_device[state] == torch.device(\"cpu\")` holds for everything, yet the buffer was never moved anywhere — the assertion fails for every parametrization that includes it.\n\nThis is the device-state counterpart of #8409, which gated the **memory-delta** assertions on `allocator_backed_memory_stats` for the same reason. Add a sibling `offload_leaves_accelerator` flag (next to that one) and require it for the removal check.\n\n## Behavior\n\n| Accelerator | `offload_leaves_accelerator` | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| CUDA / XPU | `True` | assertion unchanged, bit for bit |\n| cpu | `False` | state falls through to the device comparison instead, which holds |\n\n## Validation (executed on real hardware)\n\n20-core x86_64 CPU without AVX512-FP16, gloo backend, 2 ranks (`LOCAL_SIZE=2`), `-n 4`:\n\n- **Without** this change on master: `16 failed, 112 passed` — and all 16 failures are this single assertion (`State OffloadStateTypeEnum.contiguous_grad_buffer must be removed after offload_states()`).\n- **With** this change: `128 passed, 0 failed`.\n\nSo the cpu accelerator keeps the coverage of the other 112 parametrizations instead of losing them to a blanket skip.\n\nThe enclosing cpu CI run is #8381, which exercises this file at multi-rank and reports the same result (`128 PASSED`).",
    "changed_files": 1,
